              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Cassandra"
            },
            "reviewBody": "people always call this a finnish pancake, it's closer to french toast. a family fav. just heat the pan first. it's fantastic with syrup.from: a finnish girl. SISU!!!"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "hooked on recipes"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Ok, you need more flour...more like 1.5 C and half the butter. Sugar depends on your personal taste. I'm Finnish, trust me...this is outstanding but the typical pannukakku batter is thicker than a lettu batter...let the batter sit for 15-20 min white preheating the oven.",
            "datePublished": "2021-06-27T15:44:51.633Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "ChirpyBun8010"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Hi! I usually add freshly ground cardamom seeds and sometimes even vanilla to the batter. I put some butter in the baking pan and heat it in the oven when it's warming up. Tilt the baking pan so that the butter covers the bottom of it. It gives a wonderful flavor. For a savory pannukakku, omit the sugar in the published recipe and sprinkle pieces of bacon on it before baking it. It is called läskipannukakku, My Finnish mother loved it but I don't care so much for it. I prefer the sweet one with some strawberry jam. Läskipannukakku is served with lingonberry jam..",
            "datePublished": "2026-05-09T15:58:43.683Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Tim Golisch"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I used half of the recommended sugar and added 1/4 tsp vanilla. I wisked the ingredients on high to get it mixed well. 20 minutes at 400F.",
            "datePublished": "2021-01-01T16:12:47.977Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "TROUZER"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This turned out great for me! I scaled the recipe down to 2 servings and added 1/4 t. of vanilla, but that is the only change I made. I used a 7-inch cast iron skillet and my toaster oven set on 400 degrees to bake. It took exactly 15 minutes to bake. Topped with tart cherry preserves, it was a tasty breakfast with a navel orange and coffee. Thanks for the recipe!",
            "datePublished": "2012-02-26T06:46:46.460Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Beth Bucek"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Way way to salty for my taste! Unless using unsalted butter I would omit the salt all together next time. There is plenty in the butter to salt this. I would also reduce the butter to 6 TBS. Other then than great recipe!",
            "datePublished": "2014-06-05T10:00:19.260Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "HONEYWINE01"
            },
            "reviewBody": "It may be technique, as it was the first time I prepared this, but it turned out a little differently than the pannu kakku that I've eaten in Michigan's U.P. It was a little less custardy than I'm accustomed to, and did not puff up and then \"fall\".\nIt was, however, very easy to do and absolutely delicious as it turned out. After looking at some versions on Scandinavian cooking I added vanilla and lemon zest.",
            "datePublished": "2015-03-01T19:23:11.583Z"
          },
          {
